# ---------------------------------------------------------------- CITIES
CITIES = [
("anaheim","Anaheim","Anaheim has some of the county's largest stock of 1950s and 60s single-story tracts, many held by original owners for decades. Probate sales here draw strong interest from first-time buyers and small builders, and homes near the Colony district and the Anaheim Hills side sell to very different buyer pools."),
("irvine","Irvine","Irvine estates almost always involve an HOA, and many older villages sit on Irvine Company land with ground-lease histories buyers will ask about. HOA transfer documents and any lease terms need to be pulled early. Demand is deep and consistent, so well-presented probate homes here rarely sit."),
("santa-ana","Santa Ana","Santa Ana has the county's oldest housing, including many pre-1940 homes with unpermitted additions and multiple families on one lot. Disclosure and a pre-listing inspection matter more here than anywhere else in the county, and the buyer pool includes many owner-occupants looking for a multigenerational house."),
("huntington-beach","Huntington Beach","Coastal tracts from the 1960s and 70s, many with original owners. Probate homes within a mile or two of the beach draw both families and builders planning a rebuild, and the price gap between the two is often the deciding factor for heirs. Flood zone and coastal commission questions come up west of Pacific Coast Highway."),
("fullerton","Fullerton","Fullerton has a large share of mid-century homes on generous lots, plus older neighborhoods near downtown with character homes that attract renovators. Estates here often include garages and yards full of a lifetime's belongings; the clean-out is usually the longest part of the process."),
("garden-grove","Garden Grove","Compact postwar tracts, many with additions, and a diverse buyer pool that includes many multigenerational families. Probate homes here sell quickly when priced to condition. Check for unpermitted work before listing."),
("orange","Orange","Old Towne Orange has historic homes with preservation rules that affect what buyers can change; the rest of the city is 1960s and 70s tracts with strong owner-occupant demand. Estates near Chapman University sometimes draw investor interest for student housing, which affects how the house should be marketed."),
("costa-mesa","Costa Mesa","Home to the Costa Mesa Justice Center, where every Orange County probate hearing takes place. The city's own housing ranges from Eastside cottages on large lots, popular with builders, to Mesa Verde tracts that sell to families. Lot value often exceeds house value here, which changes the pricing conversation."),
("mission-viejo","Mission Viejo","Master-planned 1970s and 80s neighborhoods, most with HOAs and many with lake or association amenities that need transfer paperwork. Probate homes here are usually in reasonable condition and sell to move-up families; the main question is whether a light refresh pays off, and it sometimes does."),
("newport-beach","Newport Beach","High values mean the numbers in a probate sale are larger: probate referee fees, statutory attorney fees, and the stakes of the pricing decision all scale with the estate. Confirmation-hearing overbids are more common here because investors watch high-value filings. Full authority is worth petitioning for."),
]

